{
  "id": "proj-workbench-with-front-vise",
  "slug": "workbench-with-front-vise",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Workbench with Front Vise",
  "summary": "A laminated-top bench with a leg vise and dog holes. The last workbench you will need.",
  "difficulty": "advanced",
  "estimatedHours": 16,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Stack the lumber indoors with spacers between the boards for a week so it reaches shop moisture before you cut. Laminations glued wet will cup later.",
    "Cut sixteen 2x4s to 74 in. Rip the rounded edges off each one so the glue faces are flat.",
    "Glue the top in four sections of four boards each, with the boards on edge and pipe clamps every 12 in, alternating above and below. Scrape off the squeeze-out once it turns rubbery. Joint the mating faces of the sections, then glue the sections together into one 72 x 22 in slab.",
    "Flatten the top with the jack plane, working diagonally across the grain and then with it. Check with a straightedge in several directions. Trim both ends square to 72 in.",
    "Cut four legs from the 4x4s, leaving the bench height as the distance from the floor to your pinkie knuckle minus the top's thickness (usually 34 to 36 in overall). Connect the legs with 2x6 stretchers fixed by 3/8 in hex bolts so the base can be knocked down. Set the front left leg flush with the front edge of the top, because the vise clamps against it.",
    "Glue two 2x8 offcuts into a 3 in thick chop the same length as the leg. Bore the hole for the bench screw through the chop and the leg about 8 in below the top, using the hole size the screw maker specifies, and bolt the screw nut to the back of the leg.",
    "Cut a 2x4 parallel guide, mortise it into the bottom of the chop, and run it through a slot in the leg. Drill a row of 3/8 in holes through the guide 1 in apart and use the steel rod as the pin. Set the pin so the chop is parallel to the leg when the jaws are closed on your work.",
    "Bore 3/4 in dog holes 4 in in from the front edge every 6 in along the length, keeping the bit plumb with a square.",
    "Wipe on three coats of boiled linseed oil, wiping off the excess. Lay the oily rags flat outdoors to dry or soak them in water before disposal, because they can self-ignite."
  ],
